<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\noautomath</span><span id="p1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJKencfamily</span>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p">UTF8mc</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Implementing a Logical Inference System
<br class="ltx_break">for Japanese Comparatives</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yosuke Mikami
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Tokyo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Riken
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ymikami@is.s.u-tokyo.ac.jp</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Daiki Matsuoka
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Tokyo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Riken
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">daiki.matsuoka@is.s.u-tokyo.ac.jp</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hitomi Yanaka
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Tokyo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Riken
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hyanaka@is.s.u-tokyo.ac.jp</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Natural Language Inference (NLI) involving comparatives is challenging because it requires understanding quantities and comparative relations expressed by sentences.
While some approaches leverage Large Language Models (LLMs), we focus on logic-based approaches grounded in compositional semantics, which are promising for robust handling of numerical and logical expressions.
Previous studies along these lines have proposed logical inference systems for English comparatives.
However, it has been pointed out that there are several morphological and semantic differences between Japanese and English comparatives.
These differences make it difficult to apply such systems directly to Japanese comparatives.
To address this gap, this study proposes ccg-jcomp, a logical inference system for Japanese comparatives based on compositional semantics.
We evaluate the proposed system on a Japanese NLI dataset containing comparative expressions.
We demonstrate the effectiveness of our system by comparing its accuracy with that of existing LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Natural Language Inference (NLI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowman et al. 2015</a>)</cite> is the task of determining the entailment relation between premise and hypothesis sentences.
In particular, this paper focuses on inferences involving comparative expressions (e.g., <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">heavier</span>, where the comparative morpheme <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-er</span> is attached).
In (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), for example, the premise (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) entail the hypothesis (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
John is heavier than Bob.
<span id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
Bob is heavier than 70 kg.
<span id="S1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
John is heavier than 70 kg.
<span id="S1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\sn</span> (entailment)


Inferences involving comparatives like (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are challenging to an NLI system because the system needs to correctly understand the meaning of the quantity expression “70 kg” and the comparative relation between John’s and Bob’s weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are two main approaches to NLI.
One is a deep learning (DL)-based approach.
Large Language Models (LLMs), such as GPT-4o,<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          <a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-system-card/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-system-card/</a></span></span></span> have been performing accurately in various tasks, including NLI.
However, recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">She et al. (2023)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parmar et al. (2024)</a></cite> have pointed out that even such models have difficulties in handling problems involving logical connectives such as negation and quantification.
This fact indicates that DL-based models still have room for improvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The other approach to NLI is a logic-based approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abzianidze (2015)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mineshima et al. (2015)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernardy and
Chatzikyriakidis (2017)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2020)</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernardy and
Chatzikyriakidis (2021)</a></cite>, in which mathematical logic is utilized to perform NLI involving various logical expressions robustly.
In particular, inference systems based on compositional semantics have achieved high performance on NLI problems composed of lexical, syntactic, and semantic phenomena.
As for comparatives, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haruta et al. (2022)</a></cite> proposed a logical inference system for English comparatives based on <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Combinatory Categorial Grammar</span> (CCG, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steedman 2000</a></cite>) and <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">degree semantics</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cresswell 1976</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klein 1980</a>)</cite>.
However, we cannot apply the system directly to Japanese comparatives because of morphological and semantic differences between Japanese and English comparatives, which we will describe in detail in <a href="#S4" title="4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Sentence</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Semantic Representation</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">John is heavy.</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S1.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">John is heavier than 70 kg.</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land d&gt;70\text{kg}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>kg</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land d&gt;70\text{kg}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">John is heavier than all the student.</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall x.(\mathsf{student}(x)\rightarrow\exists d.(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(x,d)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗌𝗍𝗎𝖽𝖾𝗇𝗍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x.(\mathsf{student}(x)\rightarrow\exists d.(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(x,d)))</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Basic semantic representations for comparatives</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13734v1/system_overview_english.drawio.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/193;" width="429" height="193" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the proposed system</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we aim to develop a logical inference system for Japanese comparatives based on CCG and degree semantics.
Inspired by the logical inference system for English comparatives proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haruta et al. (2022)</a></cite>, our system, named ccg-jcomp, compositionally derives the semantic representations (i.e., the logical formulas representing the sentence meanings) of Japanese sentences through syntactic and semantic parsing and judges the entailment relation using a theorem prover.
Further, we implement syntactic and semantic analyses to systematically handle some phenomena specific to Japanese comparatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We experiment with JSeM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kawazoe et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, a Japanese NLI dataset containing problems involving comparatives.
We compare the performance of our system with GPT-4o and some Japanese LLMs.
Our experiment shows that our proposed system outperforms all of them in accuracy on the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compositionally derive the semantic representations of Japanese sentences containing some comparative expressions based on CCG and degree semantics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement ccg-jcomp, a logical inference system for Japanese comparatives.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                Our system is available for research use at <a href="https://github.com/ynklab/ccg-jcomp" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ynklab/ccg-jcomp</a></span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed system through experiments on a Japanese NLI dataset involving comparatives.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Degree Semantics</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our study, we adopt a theoretical framework called degree semantics, which allows us to analyze the meanings of gradable adjectives and comparatives formally.
Its basic idea is to treat a gradable adjective as a binary predicate that takes an entity and a degree as arguments.
For instance, “John is <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> feet tall” can be represented as <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{tall}(\mathsf{john},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗅𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{tall}(\mathsf{john},d)</annotation></semantics></math> (for simplicity, we omit units such as “feet”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We handle comparatives following the so-called A-not-A analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seuren 1973</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klein 1982</a>)</cite> in degree semantics.
According to this analysis, (<a href="#S2" title="2 Degree Semantics ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) can be represented as (<a href="#S2" title="2 Degree Semantics ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which means that there exists a degree <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> such that John’s weight is more than or equal to <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and Bob’s weight is not.
<span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
John is heavier than Bob.
<span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{bob},d)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖻𝗈𝖻</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{bob},d)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.T1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows some other examples of basic constructions involving comparatives and their semantic representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>System Overview</h2>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
