<td id="S3.T2.2.6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda E\ Q\ V.V(\lambda A\ F\ x.Q(\lambda I.I," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda E\ Q\ V.V(\lambda A\ F\ x.Q(\lambda I.I,</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S3.T2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda y.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land F(y&lt;d))))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda y.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land F(y&lt;d))))</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Examples of basic semantic templates</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the overview of ccg-jcomp, our proposed system.
The overall system flow follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haruta et al. (2022)</a></cite>: CCG syntactic parsing, tree conversion, semantic parsing, and theorem proving.
In what follows, we describe the details of each step, deferring the explanation of the specifics of Japanese comparatives until <a href="#S4" title="4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Syntactic Parsing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, a tokenizer tokenizes the input sentences, and a CCG parser converts them into CCG trees.
CCG is a grammar formalism that assigns a syntactic category to each grammatical expression.
The set of syntactic categories is defined recursively as follows: (i) atomic categories: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑁𝑃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{NP}</annotation></semantics></math> (noun phrase), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (sentence), etc., (ii) functional categories: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\backslash Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\backslash Y</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are syntactic categories).
Both <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\backslash Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\backslash Y</annotation></semantics></math> take the category <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> as an argument and return the category <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
“<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="/" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>/</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">/</annotation></semantics></math>” and “<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\backslash" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>\</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\backslash</annotation></semantics></math>” indicate that the argument is taken from the right and left, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CCG parsers generally use CCGbank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hockenmaier and Steedman 2007</a>)</cite> or its modified versions for training, which are not necessarily compatible with comparatives.
Thus, the output CCG trees are not always the ones we expect at this point.
To deal with this issue, we modify the CCG trees if necessary.
Another possible way to modify CCG trees is to revise the CCG parser itself.
However, this method is costly because it requires re-training or fine-tuning the CCG parser.
Thus, we leave this approach for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Semantic Parsing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this step, we assign a semantic representation to each lexical item of the CCG tree based on the semantic templates.
Then, the semantic representation of the whole sentence is composed according to the CCG rules.
To illustrate, we show two rules below.
Some other rules are provided in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Combinatory Rules of CCG ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Forward functional application rule
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-12.4pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X/Y}:{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X/Y}:{f}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">   </span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Y}:{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Y}:{a}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:81.6pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:81.6pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"> <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">    <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}:{f\,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}:{f\,a}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Backward functional application rule 
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-12.4pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Y}:{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Y}:{a}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">   </span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X\backslash Y}:{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X\backslash Y}:{f}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:81.8pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:81.8pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"> <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">    <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}:{f\,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}:{f\,a}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We set up the semantic templates in order to give semantic representations to the lexical items.
<a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the semantic templates for basic comparative expressions.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            For expository purposes, the semantic templates listed here are simplified from the original ones, which are more complicated in order to handle various expressions.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us proceed to some details of the templates in <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, focusing on the function <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> that appears in the templates for positive/negative adjectives, which we have newly added to handle comparatives.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>) represents the surface form of the word (resp. the generalized quantifier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barwise and Cooper 1981</a>)</cite>).
</span></span></span>
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> has five arguments, the first one <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> being the base form of the adjective, and the fifth one <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> being the subject of the adjective.
Turning to the second argument <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda d.d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda d.d</annotation></semantics></math>, it is introduced for the differential comparatives.
Consider (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) for example.
In the semantic composition process, this argument becomes <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda d.(d+5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda d.(d+5)</annotation></semantics></math> as a result of the combination of “5 kg” and the adjective “omoi” (heavy), which leads to the intended semantic representation (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Taro-wa Jiro yori 5 kg omoi. (Taro is 5 kg heavier than Jiro.)
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall d.(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d+5))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall d.(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d+5))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The third argument, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda d.d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda d.d</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda d.{-d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda d.{-d}</annotation></semantics></math>), indicates whether the adjective is positive or negative.
This allows us to distinguish between (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), which contain adjectives of the opposite polarity.
For instance, by assuming that “karui” (light) is a negative adjective, we can derive the semantic representation (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) for (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), where the argument <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda d.{-d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda d.{-d}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-5</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
Taro-wa Jiro yori 5 kg karui. (Taro is 5 kg lighter than Jiro.)
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall d.\left(\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{jiro},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{taro},d-5)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗅𝗂𝗀𝗁𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝗅𝗂𝗀𝗁𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall d.\left(\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{jiro},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{taro},d-5)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>


Similarly, the fourth argument, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t.t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t.t</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t.\lnot t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t.\lnot t</annotation></semantics></math>), makes a distinction about the polarity of the adjectives in comparatives with measure phrases.
Taking (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Semantic Parsing ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) for example, the arguments <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t.t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t.t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t.\lnot t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t.\lnot t</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;70</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lnot(d&gt;70)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lnot(d&gt;70)</annotation></semantics></math> in the semantic representations, respectively.
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.8" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.9" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>Taro-wa 70 kg yori omoi. (Taro is heavier than 70 kg.)
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.10" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span><math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d)\land d&gt;70\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d)\land d&gt;70\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.11" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.12" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.13" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>Taro-wa 70 kg yori karui. (Taro is lighter than 70 kg.)
<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.14" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span><math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists d.\left(\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{taro},d)\land\lnot(d&gt;70)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗅𝗂𝗀𝗁𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists d.\left(\mathsf{light}(\mathsf{taro},d)\land\lnot(d&gt;70)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Theorem Proving</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this step, we input the logical formulas of the premises and hypothesis obtained in the previous step into an automated theorem prover and judge their entailment relation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Axioms</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prove entailment relations, we introduce some axioms.
To illustrate, we describe one of the axioms, (CP), which is shown below (here, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is an adjective).
It corresponds to a basic axiom in degree semantics called Consistency Postulate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klein 1980</a>)</cite>.</p>
<dl id="S3.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(CP)</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} &amp;\forall x\ y.\left(\left(\exists d.\left(\mathbf{A}(x,d)\land\lnot\mathbf{A}(y,d)\right)\right)\right.\\
&{}\rightarrow\forall d.\left.\left(\mathbf{A}(y,d)\rightarrow\mathbf{A}(x,d)\right)\right)\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} &amp;\forall x\ y.\left(\left(\exists d.\left(\mathbf{A}(x,d)\land\lnot\mathbf{A}(y,d)\right)\right)\right.\\
&amp;{}\rightarrow\forall d.\left.\left(\mathbf{A}(y,d)\rightarrow\mathbf{A}(x,d)\right)\right)\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, this axiom requires that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a predicate such that if the degree of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than the degree of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, then the degree of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than or equal to the degree of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using this axiom, we can make inferences such as (<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We give the details of the proof in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details of Axioms ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, where we also explain other axioms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we choose some axioms based on the adjectives in the input sentences and add them as premises.
Then, we input the logical formulas of the premises and hypothesis into the automated theorem prover.
Given the premises and axioms <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\ldots P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\ldots P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the hypothesis <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, the system output is <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">yes</span> (entailment) when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\land\dots\land P_{n}\rightarrow H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\land\dots\land P_{n}\rightarrow H</annotation></semantics></math> is proven, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no</span> (contradiction) when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\land\dots\land P_{n}\rightarrow\lnot H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\land\dots\land P_{n}\rightarrow\lnot H</annotation></semantics></math> is proven, and <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span> (neutral) when neither is proven.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we explain some linguistic phenomena specific to Japanese comparatives and how we treat them in this study.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">English has overt comparative morphemes, such as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-er</span>.
On the other hand, Japanese has no such morphemes.
The examples (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) illustrate that the adjective “omoi” has the same surface form whether it is used for comparison or not.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span> <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\gll</span>Taro-wa Jiro yori <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">omoi</span>.
<br class="ltx_break">Taro-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">top</span> Jiro than heavy
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\glt</span>“Taro is heavier than Jiro.”
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span> <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\gll</span>Taro-wa <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">omoi</span>.
<br class="ltx_break">Taro-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">top</span> heavy
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\glt</span>“Taro is heavy.”


Although it is possible to give different semantic representations to “omoi” in both sentences, we assign the same semantic representation to simplify the semantic parsing process.
Accordingly, we introduce an unpronounced symbol (empty category) to distinguish the semantic representations of the two sentences.
Specifically, when there is no comparative expression such as “…yori” and “…izyoo-ni,” we insert an empty category <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cmp</span> of category <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S/S</annotation></semantics></math> instead.
We introduce the aforementioned comparison criterion <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by assigning the following semantic representation (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) to this empty category.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda S.S(\lambda A\ x.A(x,\theta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda S.S(\lambda A\ x.A(x,\theta))</annotation></semantics></math>

This inserted operator also plays a role of matching the types of the semantic representations of “Jiro yori omoi” and “<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cmp</span> omoi” (<a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.1 Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Absence of Overt Comparative Morphemes ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-44.6pt;">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">   <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Jiro</span> </span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:56.8pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:56.8pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.F3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}{\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda P.P(\mathsf{jiro})}}\end{matrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝑁𝑃</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}{\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda P.P(\mathsf{jiro})}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">   </span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_p">         <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">yori (than)</span> </span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:212.6pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:212.6pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}{(S/S)\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q\,S.S(\lambda A\,x.Q(\lambda y.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land\lnot A(y,d))))}}\end{matrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>𝑁𝑃</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}{(S/S)\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q\,S.S(\lambda A\,x.Q(\lambda y.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land\lnot A(y,d))))}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:283.8pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:283.8pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-2.0pt;"> <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">      <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}{S/S}\\
{{}:{\lambda S.S(\lambda A\,x.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land\lnot A(\mathsf{jiro},d)))}}\end{matrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}{S/S}\\
{{}:{\lambda S.S(\lambda A\,x.\exists d.(A(x,d)\land\lnot A(\mathsf{jiro},d)))}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">   </span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_p">    <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">omoi (heavy)</span> </span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:121.6pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:121.6pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}{S\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q\,N.Q(\lambda x.N(\mathsf{heavy},x))}}\end{matrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>𝑁𝑃</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}{S\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q\,N.Q(\lambda x.N(\mathsf{heavy},x))}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">      <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.5pt;"></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:378.7pt;"><span class="ltx_rule ltx_filled_leader" style="width:378.7pt;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span><span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.8pt;"> <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{&gt;}\mathbf{B}_{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>×</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{&gt;}\mathbf{B}_{\times}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">               <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"> <span id="S4.F3.2.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}{S\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q.Q(\lambda x.\exists d.(\mathsf{heavy}(x,d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d)))}}\end{matrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>𝑁𝑃</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}{S\backslash\mathit{NP}}\\
{{}:{\lambda Q.Q(\lambda x.\exists d.(\mathsf{heavy}(x,d)\land\lnot\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d)))}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> </span></span>
</span></span></p></div></div>
