</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Equatives</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">English equative sentences such as (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) are interpreted as indicating “… is at least as heavy as … .”
Thus, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haruta et al. (2022)</a></cite> represented (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) as (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span> John is as heavy as Bob.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{bob},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖻𝗈𝖻</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗁𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall d.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{bob},d)\rightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{john},d)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>


On the other hand, Japanese equatives merely express that the degrees are close to each other. For instance, (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) can be true even when Taro’s weight is slightly less than Jiro’s.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{xlist}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\gll</span>Taro-wa Jiro to onaji kurai-no omosa-da.
<br class="ltx_break">Taro-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">top</span> Jiro  same as-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">gen</span> weight-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">cop</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\glt</span>“Taro is as heavy as Jiro.”
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\gll</span>Jiro-wa omoi.
<br class="ltx_break">Jiro-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">top</span> heavy
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\glt</span>“Jiro is heavy.”
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\gll</span>Taro-wa omoi. (entailment)
<br class="ltx_break">Taro-<span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_smallcaps">top</span> heavy
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\glt</span>“Taro is heavy.”


To handle the meaning of equatives, we propose the following representation (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) for (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
This intuitively indicates that the difference in weight between Taro and Jiro is less than the constant <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} &amp;\forall d_{1}d_{2}.\left(\left(\lnot\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d_{1})\right.\right.\right.\\
&{}\leftrightarrow\left.\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d_{1})\right)\\
&{}\land\left.\lnot\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d_{2})\leftrightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d_{2})\right)\right)\\
&{}\rightarrow\left.|d_{1}-d_{2}|&lt;\delta\right)\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>¬</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo>¬</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗍𝖺𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗃𝗂𝗋𝗈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} &amp;\forall d_{1}d_{2}.\left(\left(\lnot\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d_{1})\right.\right.\right.\\
&amp;{}\leftrightarrow\left.\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d_{1})\right)\\
&amp;{}\land\left.\lnot\left(\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{taro},d_{2})\leftrightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(\mathsf{jiro},d_{2})\right)\right)\\
&amp;{}\rightarrow\left.|d_{1}-d_{2}|&lt;\delta\right)\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math>

We also introduce the following axiom (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), which prescribes the relation between <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, this axiom indicates that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is so small that the truth value of the predicate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{heavy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{heavy}</annotation></semantics></math> does not change within the range of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{exe}</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.21" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\ex</span>
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(x,\theta-\delta)\leftrightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(x,\theta+\delta)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁𝖾𝖺𝗏𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x.\left(\mathsf{heavy}(x,\theta-\delta)\leftrightarrow\mathsf{heavy}(x,\theta+\delta)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

We can make inferences such as (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Equatives ‣ 4 Challenges in Handling Japanese Comparatives ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) using this axiom together with (UP) and (DOWN) (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details of Axioms ‣ Implementing a Logical Inference Systemfor Japanese Comparatives" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Clausal Comparatives</h3>

